  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through next Sunday)...\n\nFirst thing, wildfire smoke will likely continue to impact our \nregion for the foreseeable future, keeping skies quite hazy, with \nreduced surface visibilities and poor air quality at times.\n\nSatellite imagery shows the relatively stagnant upper flow \npattern over the western and central CONUS, with a dome of high \npressure centered over the southern Rockies and relatively flat \nwesterly flow over our area. Monsoon moisture continues to be \nsuppressed to the south of our CWA. However, the pattern generally\nkeeps low level flow easterly. Models continue to indicate low \nlevel moisture advection from the plains into our region this \nweek. Some decent moisture will reside along the Dakota borders \nthrough Tuesday with a slight chance of thunderstorms in our \nsoutheast. Then by Wednesday, ensembles agree PWATs will be \ntrending upward quite a bit as that easterly flow is able to tap \ndeep moisture from the Great Plains. PWATs over an inch are \nprogged as far west as Billings by late Wednesday. A trough \ndropping into the Pacific NW at midweek will gradually shift our \nmid level flow to the southwest opening the door to Pacific and \nmonsoonal moisture along with related impulses. Thus, our PoPs \njump up to 30-70% across the region by Wednesday evening and \ncontinues through the end of the week. Storms should be capable \nof locally heavy rainfall due to the anomalously high PWATs for \nthis time of year. \n\nTiming of waves and periods of ascent/subsidence into late week \nare difficult to determine now, but generally speaking ensembles \nsuggest moisture will remain elevated and the risk of t-storms \nwill continue into the weekend. \n\nTemperatures will transition from above normal early this week to\nbelow normal at the end. Look for upper 80s to mid 90s Monday and\nTuesday, followed by 80s Wednesday, then 70s to lower 80s \nThursday through the weekend. BT",
